#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060975 :tutorial/content "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. In particular, we determine how long or short an object is by measuring its **length**. We use **length units** to specify an object's length.\n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit of length is the **meter,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{m}$.\" Lengths are measured using **meter sticks** like the one below.\n\n![](images/58/58c549aa0dfda587c3d2423c918d12ebfd78b17ef66d44fb78cd2d8d6cb27242.png)\n\nIt's convenient to define new units of measurement from the base unit. The most common variations for length units are shown below:\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest unit $(\\textrm{km})$ to the smallest $(\\textrm{mm})$:\n\n- The world's tallest building is the Burj Khalifa in Dubai. Its height is approximately $1\\,\\textrm{km}$.\n- The length of one large stride is approximately $1\\,\\textrm{m}$.\n- The width of a standard paperclip is around $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n- The thickness of a credit card is around $1\\,\\textrm{mm}$.\n\n**Important**: Each metric symbol of length ends in \"$\\textrm{m}$\", the base unit." :tutorial/id #uuid "30df5966-0fa8-5128-8572-48d3c6b48e26" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7781 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060976 :tutorial/content "Another way to measure length is to use **customary units**. The most common customary units for length are **inch**, **foot**, **yard**, and **mile**.\n\nA table showing some of the most common customary units of length is shown below.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Mile | $\\textrm{mi}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{mi}=1760\\,\\textrm{yd}$ |\n| Yard | $\\textrm{yd}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$ |\n| Foot | $\\textrm{ft}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{ft}=12\\,\\textrm{in}$ |\n| Inch | $\\textrm{in}$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest unit $(\\textrm{mi})$ to the smallest $(\\textrm{in})$:\n\n- Four loops around a standard running track is approximately $1\\,\\textrm{mi}$.\n- The length of one large stride is approximately $1\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n- The length of an adult's foot is approximately $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- The length of an adult's thumb tip is around $1\\,\\textrm{in}$.\n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c84c981-ba09-5ead-a757-86242a922a32" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7797 :tutorial/title "Customary Units of Length"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060980 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above.\n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$.\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm c$\" stands for **centi**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{100}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{cm} = \\dfrac{1}{100}\\,\\textrm{m}$. Notice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ to itself $100$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{m}{:}$\n$1cm + 1cm + ⋯ + 1cm^(⏞)^(100times)|=; \\frac{1}{100}m + \\frac{1}{100}m + ⋯ + \\frac{1}{100}m|=; 100 \\cdot \\frac{1}{100}m|=; \\frac{100 \\cdot 1}{100}m|=; \\frac{100}{100}m|=; 1m$\nTherefore, $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m}$, as stated in the table.\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mm} = \\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\,\\textrm{m}$. This means that $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m}$, as shown in the table." :tutorial/id #uuid "4a9204c0-7ef9-5037-b2c7-b8c28f64e8c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7943 :tutorial/title "The Prefix System for Metric Units"}]}
